{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-74348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-74348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(5.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(8)Δ{x}_{2}+f(9)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $1$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈4\\cdot 1.5+6\\cdot 2.5+9.5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6+15+9.5 \\\\  & =30.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-74348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5d922245b3c40d61b2822d6926789b6b443863b1321c1e3efd3091093b1efe1"} {"question" "q-74348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-74348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "29.25"] ["math" "30.5"] ["math" "31.25"] ["math" "32.55"] ["math" "34.5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-74348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "30.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-86350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table shows the velocity of a plane during a $20$-minute trip.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{kilometers/minute})$ | $17$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nIf the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance traveled during this period has a value of  $450\\,\\text{km}$, what is the value of $k?$\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the velocity-time graph.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-86350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The area $A$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{v(5)+v(10)+v(15)+v(20)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+27+k+18)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5(k+70) \\\\  & =5k+350\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance traveled during the $20$ minute period. Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance is $450\\,\\text{km},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+350 & =450 \\\\ 5k & =450-350 \\\\ k & =\\frac{100}{5} \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-86350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5d922245b3c40d61b2822d6926789b6b443863b1321c1e3efd3091093b1efe1"} {"question" "q-86350" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-86350" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "20"] ["math" "22"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "25"] ["math" "30"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-86350" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-115736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-2,4].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $22.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-115736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nLet's set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+5+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+8)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $22$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+16 & =22 \\\\ 2k & =22-16 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-115736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5d922245b3c40d61b2822d6926789b6b443863b1321c1e3efd3091093b1efe1"} {"question" "q-115736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-115736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-115736" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "| $x$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $7$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $2$ | $k$ | $6$ |\n\nThe table above gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-1,11]$ with a regular step size. If the right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $96$, then what must be the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)+f(7)+f(11)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $7$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $k$ | $6$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(2+k+6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =(k+8)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4k+32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be  $96,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k+32 & =96 \\\\ 4k & =96-32 \\\\ k & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ k & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5d922245b3c40d61b2822d6926789b6b443863b1321c1e3efd3091093b1efe1"} {"question" "q-346442" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346442" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the area under the curve $y=(10-2x)^{2}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$The estimated area under the curve $y=(10-2x)^{2}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/assets/1cb4c2c69c93117bd5eed39322059a3c0af84d16b293ccfc053c677ac0b6127d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2.5)+f(3)+f(3.5)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $2.5$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $25$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+16+9+4)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =54\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{decreasing}$ over the interval $[2,4],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5d922245b3c40d61b2822d6926789b6b443863b1321c1e3efd3091093b1efe1"} {"question" "q-346467" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346467" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "25"] ["math" "26.50"] ["math" "27"] ["math" "28"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346467" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "27"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346467" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346467" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "decreasing"] ["text" "increasing"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/state.json" "file_sha256" "fdefaa38effa35341357080bea956dbfc680732ab5a2c4d901b758e357de5b13"} {"question" "q-346467" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "decreasing"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-